<!-- CHAPTER 10 -->
<section class="chapter" id="chapter-10">
<h2>10. Native Applications</h2>
<p>
                        Native applications are developed
                        specifically for a particular operating
                        system using supported languages,
                        tools, and interfaces.
                    </p>
<h3>Common Technologies</h3>
<ul>
<li>Kotlin for Android</li>
<li>Java for Android</li>
<li>Swift for Apple platforms</li>
<li>Objective-C for some Apple applications</li>
</ul>
<h3>Advantages</h3>
<ol>
<li>Strong operating system integration</li>
<li>Access to supported device features</li>
<li>Good performance for many applications</li>
<li>Familiar platform-specific interfaces</li>
</ol>
<h3>Limitations</h3>
<ul>
<li>Separate development may be needed for Android and iOS.</li>
<li>Maintaining multiple versions requires resources.</li>
</ul>
</section>
<!-- CHAPTER 11 -->
<section class="chapter" id="chapter-11">
<h2>11. Web Applications</h2>
<p>
                        A web application is software accessed
                        through a web browser.
                    </p>
<p>
                        Web applications commonly use HTML,
                        CSS, and JavaScript and may communicate
                        with servers to process information.
                    </p>
<h3>Advantages</h3>
<ul>
<li>Works across devices with compatible browsers</li>
<li>Often requires no traditional installation</li>
<li>Can be updated centrally</li>
<li>Accessible through internet or local networks</li>
</ul>
<h3>Limitations</h3>
<ul>
<li>Some features depend on internet connectivity.</li>
<li>Hardware access may be browser-dependent.</li>
<li>Performance depends on browser, network, and server.</li>
</ul>
<div class="example-box">
<strong>Example:</strong>
<p>
